mental health is one example of a data  gap there's no single study which  analyzes mental health and the outcomes  for people from  ethnic backgrounds the center on the  dynamics of ethnicity pulled together a  number of different studies to show that  the risk of being diagnosed with a  serious mental disorder  is five times higher if you're black  than if you're white  but it draws on nhs data which stopped  being recorded  10 years ago now suffering poor health  in old age is another example  20 of white british people aged 80 to 89  say they have poor health that's the  line at the bottom but look at the  yellow line this shows the same  proportion of people  from bangladeshi heritage age 50 to 59  years old say they have poor health  but there's no single study collecting  data on health  in relation to older people from  ethnically diverse  backgrounds similarly the home office's  own  racial disparity unit released this data  in march it shows how the stop and  search rate for black people  was four times higher than for white  people in london  and this increases to eight times more  likely for black people  in avon and somerset and 23  times more likely for black people in  dorset the biggest disparity in the  country but  no one is investigating why these rates  differ  so greatly scientists claim that it's  these data gaps which lead to  ineffective policies  they hope this major new research  project  will help
 

